bisectQ GAINED A processCode ERROR IN THE fbGen REPAIR PASS -- CAUSE FOUND, FIXED

THE ORIGINAL SYMPTOM. On 2026-08-24 the scaffold emitter in incant/f31 and its
eleven copies was repaired for families A, B and C (commit e8e8619). Eleven of
the twelve patched files improved or held. incant/bisectQ went the wrong way,
0 -> 1 processCode errors, and it was the only one that did.

THE CAUSE, found 2026-08-25 by step 1 of this file's own NEXT block, which was
the cheapest one and was the right one: THE TWO INSERTIONS ARE NOT EQUIVALENT.
Read the same patch against the sibling that went the RIGHT way:

    git diff e8e8619^ e8e8619 -- incant/fixBisect     ADDS  `fbTermN=0;`
    git diff e8e8619^ e8e8619 -- incant/bisectQ       DOES NOT add `bqTermN=0;`

The patch gave fixBisect its counter declaration and did not give bisectQ one.

WHY A MISSING DECLARATION IS FATAL RATHER THAN COSMETIC, and this is the part
worth carrying away. An UNDECLARED name in an action body becomes an action
LOCAL -- aCTionNamE mints it with isLocal set -- and parseRule/processAction
CLEAR locals on entry. So `bqTermN` inside `bqBump` and `bqTermN` inside
`bqGen` are TWO DIFFERENT NODES, each cleared per invocation. The bumper
increments its own copy; the reader never sees it move.

Everything downstream follows from that one fact:
  - `bqTermN + 1` reads a node with no data, which under bear-trap #26 returns
    its own TAG, so the runtime says `ERROR Operator + failed on bqTermN and
    Token` -- and the word `Token` in that message IS the tag coming back.
  - `bqTermN` stays 0 in bqGen, so `if bqTermN == 0; bqTagMatch(argument);`
    fires on every rule instead of only on term-less ones.
  - bqTagMatch is therefore CALLED for the first time, is lazily compiled at
    that moment, and its compile fails in that context -- which is the single
    `ERROR processCode` the whole row was about.

⚠ AND fixBisect IS NOT RIGHT, IT IS NOT-YET-WRONG. Its counter works, so
fbTagMatch is never called, so it is never compiled and never exercised. The
green row is green by not running the code. A first draft of this note claimed
fbTagMatch carried the same latent PARSE defect; that was MEASURED AND IS
FALSE -- the identical body compiles and emits `mNamE();` cleanly in isolation.
The failure is context-dependent, and what it is context-dependent ON has not
been chased. Captured, not chased.

NEXT: REMEDY -- BEHAVIOUR

THE FIX, LANDED. One line in incant/bisectQ's define block, beside bqMode:

    bqTermN=0;

Site: incant/bisectQ, the `define` block. Nothing else changed, and no C++
was touched.

TAKEN vs NOT, measured both ways in one sitting:

    remedy ABSENT    processCode errors 1   Operator+ errors 4   exit 0
    remedy PRESENT   processCode errors 0   Operator+ errors 0   exit 0

    ~/bin/incant incant/bisectQ 2>&1 | grep -c 'ERROR processCode'      -> 0
    ~/bin/incant incant/bisectQ 2>&1 | grep -c 'Operator + failed'      -> 0
    ~/bin/incant incant/bisectQ  prints TARGET ok and BISECTQ SENTINEL, exit 0

BEHAVIOUR, not assertability: the counter now actually increments, so the
emitter stops calling bqTagMatch on rules that have terms. The machine does
something different.

WHAT THE LIVE REGION ABOVE IS FOR. It no longer points at the subject -- it
demonstrates the MECHANISM, so it survives bisectQ being edited or retired,
and so it can promote into the fleet as the regression test for the trap. The
declared arm is the anti-vacuity control: a run where BOTH arms read 0 is a
broken instrument, not a passing test.

THE GENERAL HAZARD, offered for minting and not minted here, because numbering
is Tony's. It is bear-trap #38's twin one construct over:

    #38          never declare an ITERATE CURSOR -- declaring it walks 1 member
    this         never SHARE a per-invocation accumulator across two actions
                 without declaring it -- undeclared, each action gets its own

Both punish the tidy spelling and both fail silently with a plausible number.
Independently measured the same day in IncantForms/WorkingOn/parser, where the
OPPOSITE arm bites: `notFirstTimeThru` there must stay UNDECLARED, because it
is read and written inside ONE action and a define-block field would persist
across rules and emit a leading conjunct on rule 2. Same fact, both directions:

    one action, per-invocation reset wanted   -> leave it UNDECLARED
    two actions sharing one counter           -> it MUST be declared

A rule of thumb that covers both: DECLARE IT IF AND ONLY IF MORE THAN ONE
ACTION TOUCHES IT.
